my experience in a professional setting

my company is paying claude max
the amount of glazing and excitement my coworkers have/feel for AI is insane
i have not written a single line of code in my job in about 8 months
burning through tokens just to satisfy a board of directors
all of this just to end up pretty much just as efficient with my time

I'm currently in a special team at the company that is allowed to vibe code with carte blache
somehow I'm the most efficient in terms of the amount tokens used daily
i have no idea if i'm doing something special compared to my peers
I think it speeks for itself that, if you know what how you would implement it by hand,
you're gonna have a way easier time vibe coding, on top of not wasting money

so yeah, LLMs and vibe coding are here to stay. they're great tools for experienced developers that
know what they're doing and how to get there but want to speed up the process, automating the boring stuff.


---

my best experience so far with vibe coding has been in code bases that I have a
really high understanding, meaning I either wrote pretty much everything or have a
very clear picture of how I would do it myself architecture/language wise even if
I don't know use a specific language/library/framework.

so far, finding/fixing bugs, optimizations and solving js dependency hell has actually been a very good experience.

lately I've been experimenting with architecture and planning of systems design for some challeges that I'm not quite sure how to tackle
it's a good way to research and reference existing patterns that you don't know about/never used, which is something that in the past
would require me to be spending hours on google going through obscure links and documentations or code examples somehow only found in chinese or spanish (looking at you OpenCV.js).

So the Plan mode has been great for that, and from there you can edit it yourself, make your own decisions about how to structure a specific system.

